#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087478 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e429a65a-d65d-5b3f-a054-e3ab8a61caf3" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5299" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1)/((x + 2)(x - 3))\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 2)(x - 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x = -2$ and $x = 3$. Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x = -2$ and $x = 3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087479 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "f4879578-ac07-54f8-b00f-5ab651ae240a" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5301" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x^3+x^2+4x}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2}{x^{3} + x^{2} + 4x} \\\\\n&= (2)/(x(x^{2} + x + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0$.\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n$D|= b^{2} - 4ac; = (1)^{2} - 4(1)(4); = 1 - 16; =-15$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087480 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "011cf746-baa5-5395-8515-3beb98ff22a4" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5302" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{x^2-6x+8}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x - 2}{x^{2} - 6x + 8} \\\\\n&= (x - 2)/((x - 2)(x - 4)) \\\\\n&= (x - 2)/((x - 2)(x - 4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x - 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n$x - 4 = 0$\n\nThis gives the solution $x = 4$. Therefore, $x = 4$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087481 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "51ff19a3-5c58-5c26-9c29-14ffc7d284db" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5304" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-x-6}{x^3 - 3 x^2 + x - 3}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} - x - 6}{x^{3} - 3x^{2} + x - 3} \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)(x - 3))/(x^{2}(x - 3) + (x - 3)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)(x - 3))/((x^{2} + 1)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)(x - 3))/((x^{2} + 1)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 2}{x^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (0)^{2} - 4(1)(1) \\\\\n&= 0 - 4 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce48952f-e83e-5de6-8d03-4fd3925b941c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1, x =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085562} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a20e35-4bc6-501a-a719-20e146cc68df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = \\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085563} {:answer/id #uuid "06afdeec-de6d-5d71-b343-aee6d4096274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x =-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085564} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf0f315-4ac5-5e0c-859f-f25663cc6b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085565} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddb3f0d-6a42-5052-a53a-00cc70b090a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1, x = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085566}] :answer-field/correct {:answ